<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Non differentiable Continuous Function</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the following piecewise-defined function:</p>

<p>\[
f(x)=
\begin{cases}
{@f_left@}, & x<{@c@},\\
{@f_right@}, & x\geq {@c@}.
\end{cases}
\]</p>

<p><strong>(a)</strong> Calculate the left-hand and right-hand limits of \(f\) at \(x={@c@}\).</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \lim_{x\to {@c@}^-}f(x)=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle \lim_{x\to {@c@}^+}f(x)=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Calculate the value of the function at \(x={@c@}\).</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle f({@c@})=\) [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> Calculate the left-hand and right-hand derivatives of \(f\) at \(x={@c@}\).</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle f'({@c@}^-)=\) [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</p>

<p>\(\displaystyle f'({@c@}^+)=\) [[input:ans5]] [[validation:ans5]] [[feedback:prt5]]</p>

<p><strong>(d)</strong> Hence, at \(x={@c@}\), the function \(f\) is [[input:ans6]] and [[input:ans7]] [[validation:ans6]] [[validation:ans7]] [[feedback:prt6]].</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>(a)</strong> Evaluating the two branches at \(x={@c@}\) gives</p>

<p>\[
\lim_{x\to {@c@}^-}f(x)
=\left.{@f_left@}\right|_{x={@c@}}
={@ta1@},
\qquad
\lim_{x\to {@c@}^+}f(x)
=\left.{@f_right@}\right|_{x={@c@}}
={@ta2@}.
\]</p>

<p><strong>(b)</strong> Since \(x={@c@}\) belongs to the second branch,</p>

<p>\[
f({@c@})
=\left.{@f_right@}\right|_{x={@c@}}
={@ta3@}.
\]</p>

<p><strong>(c)</strong> Differentiating each branch and evaluating at \(x={@c@}\) gives</p>

<p>\[
f'({@c@}^-)
=\left.{@f_left_deriv@}\right|_{x={@c@}}
={@ta4@},
\qquad
f'({@c@}^+)
=\left.{@m@}\right|_{x={@c@}}
={@ta5@}.
\]</p>

<p><strong>(d)</strong> The one-sided limits agree with the function value:</p>

<p>\[
\lim_{x\to {@c@}^-}f(x)
=\lim_{x\to {@c@}^+}f(x)
=f({@c@})={@ta3@}.
\]</p>

<p>Thus, \(f\) is <strong>continuous</strong> at \(x={@c@}\). However, the one-sided derivatives are \(f'({@c@}^-)={@ta4@}\) and \(f'({@c@}^+)={@ta5@}\), which are unequal. Hence, \(f\) is <strong>not differentiable</strong> at \(x={@c@}\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
